---
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import katex from 'katex';
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import LunarReferences from '../../../components/LunarReferences.astro';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';
import '../../../styles/data-analysis.css';
import '../../../styles/lunar-study.css';
const base = '/data-analysis/sex-and-the-moon';
const data = JSON.parse(readFileSync(`public${base}/results.json`, 'utf8'));
const source = 'https://github.com/lasy/Seasonality-Public-Repo/tree/8ed416e7d4b30bcf73d00e69a78e236396c2b70e';
const sourceMethods = 'https://github.com/lasy/Seasonality-Public-Repo/blob/8ed416e7d4b30bcf73d00e69a78e236396c2b70e/Code/Seasonality_ANALYSIS.pdf';
const math = (s:string) => katex.renderToString(s, {displayMode:true, throwOnError:true, strict:'error', trust:false});
const p = (n:number) => n < .001 ? n.toFixed(4) : n.toFixed(3);
const pct = (n:number) => `${n > 0 ? '+' : ''}${n.toFixed(2)}%`;
---
<BaseLayout title="Sex and the Moon" description="An exploratory study of sexual-activity logging across the lunar cycle, with interactive regional estimates, uncertainty, methods and sources." pageType="article" publishedTime="2026-09-28" noindex={true}>
<LunarReferences>
<div id="lunar-study" class="analysis-page lunar-study">
  <header class="analysis-intro">
    <p class="analysis-kicker">Behavior &amp; environment · An early look at the evidence</p>
    <h1>Sex and the Moon.</h1>
    <p class="analysis-deck">Do people have more sex at certain phases of the Moon? Here’s what two years of app records can tell us.</p>
    <p class="lunar-byline">Boomer Rawlings · 28 September 2026 · Records: July 2017–June 2019</p>
  </header>
  <div class="lunar-overview">
    <p>Our main analysis found a small pattern in when people recorded sex. But the result changed when we analyzed the records in different ways. <strong>We cannot identify a reliable Moon phase when people generally have more sex.</strong> Comparing the Full Moon with the New Moon did not give a clear answer either. <a data-lunar-cite href={`${base}/model-results.csv`}>Rawlings (2026). All 21 planned model results: coefficients, covariance, contrasts, uncertainty and regional corrections. New exploratory calculations.</a></p>
    <p>The records come from Clue, an app where people can record sexual activity. Choose an area below to see what its records suggest. All five areas belong to this one dataset, so they do not give us five separate studies confirming the same result. <a data-lunar-cite href={source}>Symul and colleagues. Seasonality public data and analysis repository, frozen at commit 8ed416e7d4b30bcf73d00e69a78e236396c2b70e. Source of the selected Clue aggregates.</a></p>
  </div>
  <div class="lunar-highlights" aria-label="Principal findings">
    <div><strong>1.58% higher</strong><span>The estimated recording rate at the curve’s highest point, compared with its lowest.</span><small>This is a relative change in sex entries per user with records for that date. <a href="#explain-size">See a worked example</a>.</small></div>
    <div><strong>No clear difference</strong><span>Sex recording at Full Moon compared with New Moon, after the same calendar adjustments.</span><small>The estimate is 1.02% higher at Full Moon, but the uncertainty includes no difference. <a href="#explain-full-new">What is being compared?</a></small></div>
    <div><strong>Clue app records</strong><span>1,812,110 sex-related entries across five areas in four countries.</span><small>July 2017–June 2019. The same people can contribute repeatedly; this is not a count of 1.8 million people.</small></div>
  </div>
  <div class="analysis-actions"><a class="analysis-button" href="#explore">Explore the findings <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a><a href={`${base}/sex-and-the-moon-report.pdf`}>Read the report · PDF</a><a href="#downloads">Data and sources</a></div>
  <p class="lunar-measure"><strong>What counts as a record?</strong> An entry someone chose to make in the app. People can add entries later, or leave things out. We compare sex-related entries with the number of people who recorded anything for the same date. That helps account for changes in recording activity, but it cannot tell us every time someone had sex. <a data-lunar-cite href={sourceMethods}>Symul et al., source analysis, Seasonality_ANALYSIS.pdf: feature aggregation, birth-year bins, reporting denominators and calendar models. Pinned source version.</a> <a data-lunar-cite href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41562-020-01046-9">Pierson et al. (2021). Daily, weekly, seasonal and menstrual cycles in women’s mood, behaviour and vital signs. Nature Human Behaviour 5, 716–725. Corroborates retrospective entries in the same app; does not verify this export’s date contract.</a></p>
  <section class="lunar-weekdays" aria-labelledby="weekday-heading"><h2 id="weekday-heading">Weekday patterns are accounted for</h2><p>A Sunday can differ from a Tuesday for reasons unrelated to the Moon. We gave each area its own <strong>Monday-through-Sunday pattern</strong>, then estimated the lunar pattern after allowing for those weekly differences. The main analysis also includes seasons, changes over time and selected holidays. <a data-lunar-cite href={`${base}/analysis-plan.md`}>Rawlings (2026). Original locally frozen exploratory analysis plan, recorded before lunar outcome inspection. Not an externally registered protocol. Its original nonpublication scope describes that analysis stage.</a></p><p class="analysis-note">This helps distinguish weekly patterns from lunar ones. It does not account for every influence on sex or on what people record.</p></section>
  <details class="lunar-question" id="explain-size"><summary>What does 1.58% mean?</summary><p>We draw a smooth curve through the Moon’s cycle using the statistical model. “Fitted” means estimated by that model. The “peak” is the curve’s highest point; the “trough” is its lowest. They are not the busiest and quietest actual days in the records.</p><p>At the high point, the model estimates <strong>1.58% more sex-related entries per user with any record for that date</strong> than at the low point, after allowing for calendar patterns.</p><p><strong>An illustration:</strong> if the low point were 10 sex entries per 100 users with records for that date, the high point would be about <strong>10.16 per 100</strong>. Those are example rates, not the observed rates. A 1.58% relative rise is not an extra 1.58 entries per 100 users.</p><p>The size is uncertain: the main analysis gives a 95% interval from 0.06% to 3.26%. Other analysis choices change the evidence. This does not measure the percentage of people having sex or establish a best night. <a data-lunar-cite href={`${base}/model-results.csv`}>Rawlings (2026). All 21 planned model results: coefficients, covariance, contrasts, uncertainty and regional corrections. New exploratory calculations.</a></p></details>
  <details class="lunar-question" id="explain-full-new"><summary>What does Full Moon versus New Moon compare?</summary><p>It compares the model’s estimated <strong>sex entries per user with records for that date</strong> at two particular phases: Full Moon and New Moon. We hold the weekday, seasonal and other included calendar terms the same, so this is not simply a comparison of all raw entries on those dates.</p><p>The estimate is <strong>1.02% higher at Full Moon</strong>. But the 95% uncertainty range runs from <strong>0.47% lower to 2.54% higher</strong>. Because that range includes zero, this comparison does not give a clear answer.</p><p>This is a different question from the 1.58% figure. That figure compares the highest and lowest points anywhere on the estimated curve; those points do not have to fall at Full Moon and New Moon. <a data-lunar-cite href={`${base}/model-results.csv`}>Rawlings (2026). All 21 planned model results: coefficients, covariance, contrasts, uncertainty and regional corrections. New exploratory calculations.</a></p></details>


  <section id="explore" class="analysis-section" aria-labelledby="explore-heading">
    <div class="analysis-section-title"><span>01</span><div><h2 id="explore-heading">Explore the pattern</h2><p id="lunar-adjustment">Choose an area. The line shows our estimate across the Moon’s cycle, after allowing for weekdays, seasons and selected holidays. A wider shaded area means more uncertainty.</p></div></div>
    <fieldset class="lunar-place-controls"><legend>Choose an area</legend><div class="lunar-place-buttons"><button type="button" data-model="main_adjusted" aria-pressed="true">All five areas</button>{data.regions.map((r:any)=><button type="button" data-model={r.model} aria-pressed="false">{r.label}</button>)}</div></fieldset>
    <div class="lunar-result" id="lunar-result">
      <div aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true"><h3 id="lunar-selection">{data.models[0].label}</h3><p class="lunar-explanation" id="lunar-explanation">{data.models[0].explanation}</p></div>
      <p class="analysis-note" id="lunar-chart-measure">The chart compares sex-related entries with the number of people who recorded anything for that date.</p>
      <div class="lunar-chart-legend" aria-hidden="true"><span><i></i>Estimated pattern</span><span><i class="band-key"></i>Uncertainty around the estimate</span></div>
      <figure class="lunar-chart-wrap">
        <svg id="lunar-chart" viewBox="0 0 760 340" role="img" aria-labelledby="lunar-chart-title lunar-chart-desc"><title id="lunar-chart-title">Estimated changes in sex recording across the Moon’s cycle</title><desc id="lunar-chart-desc">Interactive chart requires JavaScript. The report and numerical table contain all estimates.</desc></svg>
        <figcaption id="lunar-chart-caption">Zero is a reference level for the estimated pattern. Above it means a higher recording measure; below it means a lower one. These percentages describe changes in that measure, not percentages of people having sex. Every view uses the same scale. <a data-lunar-cite href={`${base}/results.json`}>Rawlings (2026). Published derived estimates, regional coverage, bootstrap summary and provenance hashes. No individual or raw daily records.</a></figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="lunar-phase-control"><label for="lunar-phase">Move through the Moon’s cycle</label><output for="lunar-phase" id="lunar-phase-output">Full Moon · 0.51% above the reference level</output><input type="range" id="lunar-phase" min="0" max="360" step="1" value="180" aria-valuetext="Full Moon, 0.51 percent above the reference level" /></div>
      <p class="lunar-phase-note">Move the slider with a pointer or arrow keys to read the estimate at each phase.</p>
      <details class="analysis-details lunar-numbers"><summary>See the numbers for this view</summary>
        <div class="lunar-result-topline"><span>Estimated change from lowest to highest point: <strong id="lunar-amplitude">1.58%</strong></span><span id="lunar-p-wrap">Test across the whole cycle, <i>p</i>: <strong id="lunar-p">0.039</strong></span><span id="lunar-holm-wrap" hidden>Adjusted for five area comparisons, <i>p</i>: <strong id="lunar-holm"></strong></span></div>
        <p class="lunar-result-detail" id="lunar-contrast"><strong>Full Moon versus New Moon:</strong> +1.02%; 95% interval −0.47% to +2.54%. This range includes zero, so the comparison does not give a clear answer.</p>
        <p class="analysis-note">The shaded area is a simultaneous 95% confidence band: a way to show uncertainty around the whole estimated curve under this analysis. The chart expresses the report’s index as a percentage change from its reference: an index of 101 becomes +1%.</p>
        <p class="analysis-note">The p-value is one statistical test. Passing the usual .05 cutoff does not tell us whether a pattern is large, holds up across methods, or is caused by the Moon.</p>
        <p class="analysis-note" id="lunar-coverage">Five areas · 730 dates · each area given equal weight. These records cover about 25 Moon cycles.</p>
      </details>
      <noscript><p>Interactive controls require JavaScript. All results remain available in the table below and in the <a href={`${base}/sex-and-the-moon-report.pdf`}>report</a>.</p></noscript>
    </div>
    <details class="analysis-details" id="estimate-table"><summary>Read the estimates as a table</summary><p>These Full Moon versus New Moon intervals concern a specific contrast. The whole-cycle test above asks a different question. Regional tests are adjusted across the five areas with the Holm procedure. <a data-lunar-cite href={`${base}/model-results.csv`}>Rawlings (2026). All 21 planned model results: coefficients, covariance, contrasts, uncertainty and regional corrections. New exploratory calculations.</a></p><div class="analysis-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Regional lunar estimates"><table class="lunar-table" data-sortable><caption>All records: July 2017–June 2019. Select a heading to sort.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col" aria-sort="none"><button data-sort="text">Area</button></th><th scope="col" aria-sort="none"><button data-sort="number">Fitted swing (%)</button></th><th scope="col" aria-sort="none"><button data-sort="number">Full vs New (%)</button></th><th scope="col">95% interval (%)</th><th scope="col" aria-sort="none"><button data-sort="number">Whole-cycle p</button></th><th scope="col" aria-sort="none"><button data-sort="number">Holm p</button></th></tr></thead><tbody>{[data.models[0],...data.models.filter((m:any)=>m.model.startsWith('region:'))].map((m:any)=><tr><td><button data-model={m.model}>{m.label}</button></td><td data-value={m.fitted_peak_trough_percent}>{m.fitted_peak_trough_percent.toFixed(2)}</td><td data-value={m.full_vs_new_percent}>{pct(m.full_vs_new_percent)}</td><td>{pct(m.full_vs_new_ci_low)} to {pct(m.full_vs_new_ci_high)}</td><td data-value={m.joint_p}>{p(m.joint_p)}</td><td data-value={m.regional_holm_p??''}>{m.regional_holm_p===null?'Not applicable':p(m.regional_holm_p)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div></details>
  </section>

  <section class="analysis-section" id="interpretation" aria-labelledby="reading-heading">
    <div class="analysis-section-title"><span>02</span><div><h2 id="reading-heading">What does this mean?</h2><p>The records offer a starting point, but several things keep us from drawing a firm conclusion.</p></div></div>
    <div class="lunar-reading-grid"><article><h3>The estimated change is small</h3><p>The curve rises and falls only slightly. Most of the day-to-day variation remains unexplained by the Moon’s phase, even after accounting for the included calendar patterns. <a data-lunar-cite href={`${base}/model-results.csv`}>Rawlings (2026). All 21 planned model results: coefficients, covariance, contrasts, uncertainty and regional corrections. New exploratory calculations.</a></p></article><article><h3>The answer changes with the method</h3><p>Counting active users differently, changing how we handle nearby dates, or leaving out either California or the Northeast makes the statistical evidence weaker. A reliable finding should hold up to these kinds of checks. <a data-lunar-cite href={`${base}/model-results.csv`}>Rawlings (2026). All 21 planned model results: coefficients, covariance, contrasts, uncertainty and regional corrections. New exploratory calculations.</a></p></article><article><h3>There is no reliable “best night”</h3><p>The main curve’s highest point is after the Full Moon. But the uncertainty about its timing is too broad to identify a particular night when people are more likely to have sex. <a data-lunar-cite href={`${base}/sex-and-the-moon-report.pdf`}>Rawlings (2026). Sex and the Moon: sexual-activity logging and lunar phase. Research report, uncertainty, methods, source qualifications and numerical-audit summary.</a></p></article><article><h3>Recording habits may matter</h3><p>A lunar pattern also appears in the measure of whether people had any app entry for a date. That leaves open how much of the sex-recording result reflects what people choose to record. <a data-lunar-cite href={`${base}/model-results.csv`}>Rawlings (2026). All 21 planned model results: coefficients, covariance, contrasts, uncertainty and regional corrections. New exploratory calculations.</a></p></article></div>
    <details class="lunar-question"><summary>Why call the result uncertain if it is “statistically significant”?</summary><p>The main test passes the conventional .05 cutoff: its p-value is .039. But passing that cutoff does not tell us whether a pattern is large, holds up across methods, or is caused by the Moon. Here, several reasonable changes to the analysis weaken the evidence. The specific Full Moon versus New Moon comparison also remains unclear. An additional check that accounts for connections between nearby dates gives p = .026; it uses the same records, so it is not confirmation from a separate study. <a data-lunar-cite href={`${base}/results.json`}>Rawlings (2026). Published derived estimates, regional coverage, bootstrap summary and provenance hashes. No individual or raw daily records.</a></p></details>
    <details class="lunar-question"><summary>Were different studies averaged together?</summary><p>No. We searched 75 possible sources or groups of related sources. Only one available dataset had records with usable dates and measurements for this calculation. We combined its five areas, giving each equal weight. Other studies measured different things, lacked usable dates, reused the same records, or required access we did not have. Adding them together would not answer the same question. <a data-lunar-cite href={`${base}/sex-and-the-moon-report.pdf`}>Rawlings (2026). Sex and the Moon: sexual-activity logging and lunar phase. Research report, uncertainty, methods, source qualifications and numerical-audit summary.</a></p></details>
    <details class="lunar-question"><summary>Who is represented in these records?</summary><p>Selected Clue users in Brazil’s Central-West, France, the United Kingdom, California and the United States Northeast. The older source age band uses five-year birth-bin midpoints. It is adult-only conditional on accurate supplied birth-year bins; it is not verified exact ages or an exact “24 and older” sample. No national-population representativeness is claimed. <a data-lunar-cite href={sourceMethods}>Symul et al., source analysis, Seasonality_ANALYSIS.pdf: feature aggregation, birth-year bins, reporting denominators and calendar models. Pinned source version.</a></p></details>
    <details class="lunar-question"><summary>What would give us a clearer answer?</summary><p>A separate dataset that records both days with sex and days without it, using verified dates and permission for research use. It would also help to know about relationships, menstrual cycles, weather and other influences missing from these records. Researchers should set out the next study’s plan publicly before looking at its results. <a data-lunar-cite href={`${base}/analysis-plan.md`}>Rawlings (2026). Original locally frozen exploratory analysis plan, recorded before lunar outcome inspection. Not an externally registered protocol. Its original nonpublication scope describes that analysis stage.</a></p></details>
  </section>

  <section class="analysis-section" id="robustness" aria-labelledby="robustness-heading">
    <div class="analysis-section-title"><span>03</span><div><h2 id="robustness-heading">Does the finding hold up?</h2><p>See what happens when we change how the records are counted or analyzed.</p></div></div>
    <p>We chose these 21 versions of the analysis before seeing the first lunar result. Select a name to see its chart and explanation above. We show every version, including those that weaken the finding. One extra check added afterward is labeled separately. <a data-lunar-cite href={`${base}/analysis-plan.md`}>Rawlings (2026). Original locally frozen exploratory analysis plan, recorded before lunar outcome inspection. Not an externally registered protocol. Its original nonpublication scope describes that analysis stage.</a></p>
    <details class="analysis-details"><summary>Compare all 21 planned models</summary><div class="analysis-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Planned sensitivity models"><table class="lunar-table" data-sortable><caption>Unadjusted joint p-values; regional Holm values appear in the regional table. Fitted swings are selected amplitudes, not identical two-phase contrasts.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col" aria-sort="none"><button data-sort="text">Model</button></th><th scope="col" aria-sort="none"><button data-sort="number">Distinct dates</button></th><th scope="col" aria-sort="none"><button data-sort="number">Fitted swing (%)</button></th><th scope="col" aria-sort="none"><button data-sort="number">Whole-cycle p</button></th></tr></thead><tbody>{data.models.map((m:any)=><tr><td><button data-model={m.model}>{m.label}</button></td><td data-value={m.dates}>{m.dates}</td><td data-value={m.fitted_peak_trough_percent}>{m.fitted_peak_trough_percent.toFixed(2)}</td><td data-value={m.joint_p}>{p(m.joint_p)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div></details>
    <p class="analysis-note">Looking at one year at a time gives us fewer records and more uncertainty. A different test result does not, by itself, mean the years differ. Leaving out an area is a way to check the combined finding, not a test that one area differs from another.</p>
    <details class="lunar-question"><summary>One additional check, added after the main results</summary><p>A source audit found that the initial calendar controls omitted Brazil’s June 12 dating holiday. Adding one Brazil-only indicator gives p = .032 and a fitted swing of 1.62%; the Full Moon versus New Moon interval still includes zero. This was specified after seeing the main results and does not replace p = .039 or make the holiday controls exhaustive. <a data-lunar-cite href="https://www.mundonovo.ms.gov.br/o-dia-dos-namorados-e-comemorado-em-12-de-junho-no-brasil/">Prefeitura de Mundo Novo (2023). O Dia dos Namorados é comemorado em 12 de junho no Brasil. Primary municipal source for the June 12 observance.</a> <a data-lunar-cite href={`${base}/results.json`}>Rawlings (2026). Published derived estimates, regional coverage, bootstrap summary and provenance hashes. No individual or raw daily records.</a></p></details>
  </section>

  <details class="lunar-methods" id="methods"><summary>Research methods, equations and limitations</summary><article class="analysis-prose">
    <h2>Outcome and model</h2>
    <p>The analysis uses 3,650 balanced region-days across 730 dates, 1 July 2017–30 June 2019. All selected daily counts are positive; no pseudocount is added. The outcome is the logarithm of the reporting ratio. Balanced, unweighted ordinary least squares gives equal total weight to each regional series. <a data-lunar-cite href={`${base}/analysis-plan.md`}>Rawlings (2026). Original locally frozen exploratory analysis plan, recorded before lunar outcome inspection. Not an externally registered protocol. Its original nonpublication scope describes that analysis stage.</a></p>
    <div set:html={math(String.raw`r_{gt}=\frac{n_{gt}}{u_{gt}}`)} />
    <div set:html={math(String.raw`\log r_{gt}=\mathbf z_{gt}^{\mathsf T}\boldsymbol\gamma+a\cos\theta_{gt}+b\sin\theta_{gt}+\varepsilon_{gt}`)} />
    <dl><dt>n</dt><dd>Sex-feature logs. Multiple feature types can coexist; logs are not unique people or verified sex acts.</dd><dt>u</dt><dd>Users with any feature attributed to that date, not necessarily users opening the app that day.</dd><dt>g, t</dt><dd>Geographic area and calendar date.</dd><dt>θ</dt><dd>Lunar phase angle: New Moon 0°, first quarter 90°, Full Moon 180°, last quarter 270°. Equations evaluate angles in radians.</dd><dt>z, γ</dt><dd>Calendar controls and their coefficients: region-specific intercepts, weekdays, linear trend, two annual sine/cosine pairs and fixed holiday windows.</dd><dt>a, b, ε</dt><dd>Common lunar coefficients and the remaining model error.</dd></dl>
    <p>Holiday windows cover Christmas, New Year, Valentine’s Day, Western Easter, relevant Carnival, selected national dates and United States Thanksgiving. They are a limited set. The original plan records exact dates and windows; the June 12 addition remains separately labeled post hoc. Individual menstrual cycles, relationships, weather, artificial light and partner availability are unavailable.</p>
    <h2>What the curve and differences mean</h2>
    <div set:html={math(String.raw`I(\theta)=100\exp\!\left(a\cos\theta+b\sin\theta\right)`)} />
    <div set:html={math(String.raw`\Delta_{\mathrm{Full/New}}=100\left[\exp(-2a)-1\right]`)} />
    <div set:html={math(String.raw`\Delta_{\mathrm{peak/trough}}=100\left[\exp\!\left(2\sqrt{a^2+b^2}\right)-1\right]`)} />
    <p>The index isolates the fitted lunar component. Percentage differences are relative changes in the reporting ratio. The 95% curve band comes from the joint two-coefficient confidence ellipse; the peak-to-trough interval projects that ellipse. The Full Moon versus New Moon interval is a prespecified linear contrast on the log scale, transformed back. A fitted peak is selected from the curve; positive amplitude can be biased upward when the true signal is weak. <a data-lunar-cite href={`${base}/analysis-plan.md`}>Rawlings (2026). Original locally frozen exploratory analysis plan, recorded before lunar outcome inspection. Not an externally registered protocol. Its original nonpublication scope describes that analysis stage.</a></p>
    <h2>Serial dependence and multiplicity</h2>
    <p>The primary test jointly evaluates a = b = 0 with a two-degree-of-freedom Wald statistic. Uncertainty uses date-summed score vectors with Bartlett heteroskedasticity and autocorrelation consistent (<abbr title="Heteroskedasticity and autocorrelation consistent" tabindex="0">HAC</abbr>) covariance and 35 daily lags. Date sums allow the five areas to share date shocks. The finite-sample multiplier is T/(T − 1) × (N − 1)/(N − K), with T = 730 distinct dates, N = 3,650 region-days and K = 89 fitted parameters. <a data-lunar-cite href="https://www.statsmodels.org/stable/generated/statsmodels.stats.sandwich_covariance.cov_nw_groupsum.html">statsmodels. cov_nw_groupsum: date-grouped sandwich covariance and small-sample correction convention. Independent implementation comparator.</a></p>
    <p>The dependent wild bootstrap uses 1,999 null replicates and the same 35-day moving-average Gaussian multiplier for all areas on each date (seed 20260927). The result is p = (51 + 1)/(1,999 + 1) = .026. This is approximate calibration under time-series assumptions. It is not an exact randomized experiment. Regional tests use the Holm correction across five regions; the sensitivity family is reported in full rather than used to select a new primary model. <a data-lunar-cite href="https://publish.illinois.edu/xshao/files/2012/11/JASA-DWB.pdf">Shao (2010). The dependent wild bootstrap. Journal of the American Statistical Association 105, 218–235. Methodological basis; this study’s exact implementation is recorded in the analysis plan.</a></p>
    <h2>Astronomical assignment</h2>
    <p>Skyfield computes the apparent geocentric ecliptic longitude difference between the Moon and Sun using the National Aeronautics and Space Administration’s Jet Propulsion Laboratory (<abbr title="Jet Propulsion Laboratory" tabindex="0">JPL</abbr>) DE421 ephemeris. Representative local noon uses historical time-zone rules: São Paulo/Brasília convention, Paris, London, Los Angeles and New York. These conventions represent broad regions, not verified participant timestamps. <a data-lunar-cite href="https://rhodesmill.org/skyfield/almanac.html#phases-of-the-moon">Skyfield documentation. Phases of the Moon: apparent geocentric ecliptic longitude definition and continuous phase calculation.</a></p>
    <p>All 50 primary-phase crossings in 2018 agreed within 34 seconds with the minute-resolution times published by the United States Naval Observatory (<abbr title="United States Naval Observatory" tabindex="0">USNO</abbr>). This validates the implementation against an independent service; it does not establish subminute event accuracy. The source’s time-zone, retrospective-entry and date-shift contract remains unverified. <a data-lunar-cite href="https://aa.usno.navy.mil/data/api">United States Naval Observatory. Astronomical Applications Department API: primary Moon phases. Independent astronomical reference.</a> <a data-lunar-cite href={`${base}/phase-validation.csv`}>Rawlings (2026). All 50 timestamp comparisons between Skyfield and the published 2018 primary phases; implementation validation only.</a></p>
    <h2>Source status and verification</h2>
    <p>The original release accompanies a seasonal-reproduction medRxiv preprint. It is not an established peer-reviewed lunar study. These lunar estimates are a new exploratory analysis. A local plan was frozen before lunar outcome inspection; it was not externally preregistered. <a data-lunar-cite href="https://doi.org/10.1101/2020.11.19.20235010">Symul et al. (2022). Unmasking Seasonal Cycles in Human Fertility: How holiday sex and fertility cycles shape birth seasonality. medRxiv preprint, version 2 posted 25 January 2022; not certified by peer review.</a></p>
    <p>An independent implementation rebuilt all 21 models, calendar indicators, coefficients, covariance, intervals, regional corrections and bootstrap. The largest difference in a planned joint p-value was below 5 × 10<sup>−13</sup>; a separate statsmodels calculation also agreed. This checks computation, not self-report accuracy or completeness. The source-to-claim review was separate from the numerical audit. <a data-lunar-cite href={`${base}/numerical-audit.json`}>Rawlings (2026). Independent numerical implementation comparison: 21 planned fits and covariance/contrast checks. Computational agreement is not behavioral validation.</a></p>
  </article></details>

